<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Two entitlements the iOS app carries are deliberately absent here, and the
     reason is a measured build fact rather than a design preference.

         <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
         <array><string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)group.app.gethog</string></array>
         <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
         <array><string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)app.gethog.shared</string></array>

     Either one, alone, makes a macOS target refuse to build without a
     development certificate: "GetHogMac has entitlements that require signing
     with a development certificate" — measured by adding each separately to
     this file and building. `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM` is empty in project.yml because
     this is a public repository and a team id is an account detail (see
     DEVELOPMENT.md, "App Store archive and signing"), so a plain
     `xcodebuild build -scheme GetHogMac -destination 'platform=macOS'` has no
     certificate to offer and the target becomes unbuildable for anyone who
     clones the tree. iOS never hit this: a Simulator build is not signed
     against a profile.

     Nothing needs them yet. `KeychainTokenStore` takes its access group as an
     argument and defaults to none, and `SharedSnapshotStore` resolves the App
     Group container and falls back to the app's own when there is not one — so
     the Mac app reads and writes its own credential and its own snapshot. What
     makes a *shared* group mean anything is a second process, and there is no
     macOS widget extension. Restore both keys in the change that gives this
     target a team and something to share with. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
